    "knowledge_point": "Identifying Functions That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem",
    "problem": "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-4,4]?$\n\nI. $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}}$\n\nII. $g(x)={2}^{1/{x}^{2}}$\n\nIII. $h(x)=\\sqrt{x+4}$",
    "worked_solution": "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-4,4],$ the function must be continuous on $[-4,4]$ and differentiable on $(-4,4).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-4,4]$ and differentiable on $(-4,4).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $g(x)={2}^{1/{x}^{2}}$ is not defined at $x=0,$ hence, it is not continuous at \n$x=0,$ and so it is not continuous on $[-4,4].$ Therefore, $g(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)=\\sqrt{x+4}$ is continuous on $[-4,\\infty )$ and differentiable on $(-4,\\infty ).$ In particular, it is continuous on $[-4,4]$ and differentiable on $(-4,4).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only the functions I and III satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[-4,4].$",
